Back to the Wizard.  There's a bit of a story here.  (and there are
spoilers in it, so....)

*** SPOILERS BELOW ***






The first time we encountered da Wiz, actually it wasn't me playing
but a friend who I offered to play "for," that is, I took care of the
controls while he decided what to do.  (Actually, it was more like
playing a cooperative game, heh.)  We didn't know anything about the
Wizard except that he had the Book of the Dead, and that we needed it
to win the game.

We killed him.  Were surprised that he didn't take too long to defeat.
 Scooped up the Book.  And there was a sense that, okay, we had surely
transgressed against Nethack here, daring to do something so audacious
as KILL THE WIZARD OF YENDOR.  We didn't feel this way after defeating
the Quest nemesis, or Vlad, or any of the demon lords and princes.  A
few hundred turns later, the "You feel vaguely nervous" message
flashed across the status line.  The game was correct.

Then, shortly before the vibrating square level, imagine our horror
when, while fighting out of a cluster of unrelated nasties, another
purple-@ appeared!  "So, thou thought thou couldst kill me, fool...." 
And shortly later,  *another* purple-@ appeared *while* fighting the
first, and the twenty [mores] of attack messages that were currently
paging were followed by that pithy aside: "Double trouble."

Ever since then, I've held the Wizard in a certain regard.  Clearly,
despite the apparent ease of killing him, this is a sorcerer of
infinite cleverness, and despite the presence of stronger monsters in
the game, he certainly deserves our respect, not as a foe, but as a
singular individual.

(Okay I admit, maybe "plural" would be more appropriate.)

